1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,123
Someone shouts abuse on a bus. Someone posts a threat
about a mosque. By the time anyone reports it, the
evidence is gone.

2
00:00:11,003 --> 00:00:35,856
This is Evidence Vault — a private, on-device way to
document what happened. There's a short guided tour the
first time you open it, and it's fully skippable.

3
00:00:38,017 --> 00:00:46,298
Most of what this documents is spoken, not written — so
the main way in is video, with sound.

4
00:00:47,018 --> 00:01:00,829
It's fingerprinted the instant you stop recording — before
it's ever a file that could be edited. That fingerprint is
a SHA-256 hash: change one frame and it changes
completely.

5
00:01:02,030 --> 00:01:12,154
Only the fingerprint — never the recording — goes to four
independent OpenTimestamps calendar servers, anchored on
the Bitcoin blockchain. The recording itself never leaves
this device.

6
00:01:13,034 --> 00:01:24,088
The recording stays blurred until they choose to look, and
every field describing what happened is optional.

7
00:01:25,049 --> 00:01:33,344
They choose the category themselves. Evidence Vault ships
no AI classifier — none of this ever goes to a third-party
service.

8
00:01:34,065 --> 00:01:56,158
Then the part most tools skip: where does this actually
go? No police force accepts an automated submission, so we
don't pretend to file one. Tell it where you are, and it
shows who actually takes these reports — phone and web,
side by side.

9
00:01:58,079 --> 00:02:07,293
And for a lawyer or a court, it generates a certificate of
authenticity — written to the Federal Rules of Evidence,
or the Canada Evidence Act, depending on where you are.

10
00:02:08,094 --> 00:02:22,815
The export is five files: the report, the original
recording, the proof, the certificate, and a plain-text
cover letter.

11
00:02:24,095 --> 00:02:31,463
Every capture lands in an on-device vault — encrypted with
a PIN, never uploaded anywhere.

12
00:02:32,104 --> 00:02:44,064
Save more than one record and this app starts showing the
pattern across them — by category, by severity, timestamp
status, all in one place.

13
00:02:45,104 --> 00:02:56,148
Any past record can be re-downloaded — report, recording,
proof and certificate — any time, without ever
re-uploading anything to get it back.

14
00:02:57,108 --> 00:03:03,549
The person using this decides who else ever sees it.
Nothing here depends on this app, or this device, still
existing tomorrow.

15
00:03:04,109 --> 00:03:14,237
When pointing a camera isn't safe, audio-only capture
fingerprints a recording the same way — a call on speaker,
a voicemail, someone talking nearby.

16
00:03:15,118 --> 00:03:27,079
Only the microphone is ever used — never the camera — and
it is fingerprinted the moment you stop.

17
00:03:28,119 --> 00:03:36,859
One tap — quick exit — replaces the whole page instantly
if someone walks in.

18
00:03:37,619 --> 00:03:50,499
Anyone can independently verify the timestamp proof with
the official OpenTimestamps process — not our code — and
it keeps working even if this project disappears.

19
00:03:51,619 --> 00:04:02,659
No account. No database. No analytics. Closing the tab
deletes everything, because there was never anywhere else
for it to be.

20
00:04:03,619 --> 00:04:10,962
Thank you so much for taking the time to review Evidence
Vault — we really appreciate it.

